"# Read/Write Sets & Conflict Analysis\n\n## 1. Transaction Read/Write Sets\n\n| Txn | Read Set RS(Ti) | Write Set WS(Ti) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 | {A, B} | {A, B} | Read–modify–write on both items |\n| T2 | {A, B} | {A, B} | Read–modify–write on both items |\n| T3 | {A, B} | **∅** | Read-only |\n\n## 2. Potential Conflicts (by set intersection)\n\nSince the history fixes only per-transaction order (all three run concurrently), direction is resolved at runtime. Set-intersection tests show what *can* materialize:\n\n| Pair | RS(Ti) ∩ WS(Tj) → rw | WS(Ti) ∩ RS(Tj) → wr | WS(Ti) ∩ WS(Tj) → ww |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 ↔ T2 | {A, B} ✓ | {A, B} ✓ | {A, B} ✓ |\n| T1 ↔ T3 | {A, B} ✓ | {A, B} ✓ | ∅ ✗ |\n| T2 ↔ T3 | {A, B} ✓ | {A, B} ✓ | ∅ ✗ |\n\n**Every pair conflicts on both A and B.** T3, being read-only, can never produce a ww edge, and per item exactly **one** of wr/rw materializes (T3 reads exactly one version of each).\n\n## 3. Full Conflict Enumeration (pair × item × type)\n\n**T1 ↔ T2** (both RMW the same items — all three types, direction per interleaving):\n\n| Item | rw (Rᵢ < Wⱼ) | wr (Wᵢ < Rⱼ) | ww (Wᵢ < Wⱼ) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | T1→T2 *or* T2→T1 | T1→T2 *or* T2→T1 | T1→T2 *or* T2→T1 |\n| B | T1→T2 *or* T2→T1 | T1→T2 *or* T2→T1 | T1→T2 *or* T2→T1 |\n\n**T1 ↔ T3** and **T2 ↔ T3** (writer vs read-only — wr or rw only, mutually exclusive per item):\n\n| Pair | wr | rw |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 → T3 | W1(A) < R3(A): T3 reads from T1's version | R3(A) < W1(A): T1 overwrites after T3's snapshot |\n| T2 → T3 | W2(A) < R3(A): same, for T2 | R3(A) < W2(A): same, for T2 |\n| (item B) | identical shape | identical shape |\n\n## 4. Concrete Instantiation (canonical interleaving)\n\nOne legal global order — T2's reads land early, T1 commits first, T3 snapshots after both:\n\n```\nR2(A)₀, R2(B)₀, R1(A)₀, W1(A)₁, R1(B)₀, W1(B)₁, C1,\nW2(A)₂, W2(B)₂, C2, R3(A)₂, R3(B)₂, C3\n```\n\n| # | Ops | Type | Edge | Item |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | R2(A) < W1(A) | **rw** | T2 → T1 | A |\n| 2 | R2(B) < W1(B) | **rw** | T2 → T1 | B |\n| 3 | R1(A) < W2(A) | **rw** | T1 → T2 | A |\n| 4 | R1(B) < W2(B) | **rw** | T1 → T2 | B |\n| 5 | W1(A) < W2(A) | **ww** | T1 → T2 | A |\n| 6 | W1(B) < W2(B) | **ww** | T1 → T2 | B |\n| 7 | W2(A) < R3(A) | **wr** | T2 → T3 | A |\n| 8 | W2(B) < R3(B) | **wr** | T2 → T3 | B |\n\n(T1–T3 has **zero** conflicts in this instance: T3 reads T2's versions, not T1's. A different interleaving would shift reads-from to T1→T3 and flip directions.)\n\n## 5. Observations\n\n- **T1–T2 is the dangerous pair:** bidirectional rw edges (#1,#3 and #2,#4) close a cycle with the ww edges (#5,#6) → **G-single**, the lost-update signature. No interleaving of two concurrent RMW writers on the same items can avoid a directed cycle.\n- **T3 is cycle-safe:** its edges are all read-side (wr/rw) and cannot participate in a ww pairing; it contributes no path that closes a loop by itself.\n- **Under first-committer-wins (SI):** conflict #5/#6 cannot both survive — T2 fails validation at commit, and with it edges #1–#4 and #7–#8 are re-evaluated against T1-only history (T3 then reads from T1 instead: wr T1→T3). Only the abort of one writer makes the conflict set acyclic."